\DOC SAFE_META_EXISTS_TAC

\TYPE {SAFE_META_EXISTS_TAC : term list ref -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Strips the leading existential quantifier from the goal's conclusion, replacing
the bound variable by a fresh free variable (renamed if the original name is
already in use) and recording that variable in the supplied reference.

\DESCRIBE
The single argument {freevars : term list ref} is a reference cell in which the
tactic registers the newly introduced free variable; the observed traces show
only this role for it, and its contents are not visible in the goal state.
Applied to a goal whose conclusion is an existential statement, the tactic
performs existential elimination:
{
   A ?- ?x. t[x]
   ==============  SAFE_META_EXISTS_TAC freevars
     A ?- t[y]
}
\noindent Here {y} is {x} itself when that name is not already taken by the
assumptions or surrounding context, and a primed variant of {x} otherwise. The
assumption list is left completely unchanged, and nothing else in the
conclusion is rewritten.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single existential-elimination outcome, always on
goals whose conclusion begins with one existential quantifier, and always with
the same reference argument.

\ENDDOC
